𝑘
x×y=k
Where:
𝑥
x and 
𝑦
y are the reserves of the two assets (e.g., ETH and DAIO).
𝑘
k is a constant that must always hold for the product of the reserves.
Characteristics:
The price becomes more expensive as one asset becomes scarcer.
There is always liquidity, but the price increases exponentially as the reserve of one asset approaches zero.
Example:

This is directly integrated into Uniswap V2 or Balancer-like AMMs. This model dynamically adjusts prices as users trade one token for another.

9. Inverse Bonding Curve

An inverse bonding curve starts with a high price and decreases as more tokens are minted. This model is less common but can be useful in certain niche applications, such as when the value is tied to scarcity that diminishes over time (e.g., in deflationary ecosystems).

