Characteristics:
Price increases gradually at first, then accelerates.
Provides smoother price progression compared to an exponential curve.
Use Case:
Projects that want early participation incentives but don’t want price volatility to be as extreme as in an exponential curve.
12. Mixed or Hybrid Bonding Curves

A mixed bonding curve combines different models, using one formula in the early stages of token supply and switching to another formula as supply grows. This allows projects to optimize pricing dynamics for both early and late participants.

Example:
Early Stage: Logarithmic bonding curve to give early buyers an advantage.
Mid Stage: Linear bonding curve to stabilize prices.
Late Stage: Flat or Sigmoid curve to cap price increases and maintain long-term stability.
Characteristics:
Provides flexibility to control pricing in different phases of the token supply.
Can optimize for early growth and long-term sustainability.
Use Case:
Projects that need dynamic pricing across different stages of their lifecycle, such as a DAO launch that incentivizes early backers but maintains price stability later on.
13. Supply-Based Bonding Curve

In this model, the price is directly tied to the token supply. It offers precise control over the price-supply relationship and can be used to guarantee that a specific number of tokens are sold before a price increase occurs.

