)
P(x) is the price, 
𝑥
x is the token supply, 
𝑎
a is the initial price, and 
𝑏
b is the slope of the curve.

Use Cases:

Linear bonding curves are often used in projects where price growth should be predictable and steady without causing extreme volatility. This model is ideal for stable projects and token sales with long-term sustainability goals.

Example:

// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract LinearBondingCurve {
    uint256 public totalSupply;
    uint256 public reserveBalance;
    uint256 public constant slope = 1e18;  // Linear slope
    uint256 public constant intercept = 1e18;  // Starting price

    mapping(address => uint256) public balanceOf;

    event Buy(address indexed buyer, uint256 amount, uint256 cost);

    // Linear price curve: P(x) = intercept + slope * totalSupply
    function priceToMint(uint256 _amount) public view returns (uint256) {
        return intercept + slope * totalSupply;
    }

    // Buy tokens using ETH
    function buy() public payable {
        uint256 tokenAmount = msg.value / priceToMint(1);
        totalSupply += tokenAmount;
        reserveBalance += msg.value;
        balanceOf[msg.sender] += tokenAmount;

        emit Buy(msg.sender, tokenAmount, msg.value);
    }

    // Withdraw ETH from the reserve
    function withdraw() external {
        uint256 amount = reserveBalance;
        reserveBalance = 0;
        payable(msg.sender).transfer(amount);
    }
}
b. Exponential Bonding Curve

An exponential bonding curve accelerates token price growth as supply increases. Early participants pay significantly less than later participants, making this model ideal for projects that want to incentivize early adoption.

