Where 
𝑎
a is the initial price, 
𝑏
b is the growth factor, and 
𝑥
x is the token supply.

Use Cases:

Exponential curves are useful in speculative projects where early investors should be rewarded for entering the market early, and the price grows rapidly as more people participate. It is especially common in DeFi projects where scarcity is highly valued.

Example:

// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract ExponentialBondingCurve {
    uint256 public totalSupply;
    uint256 public reserveBalance;
    uint256 public constant initialPrice = 1e18;  // Starting price
    uint256 public constant growthRate = 2e18;  // Growth rate

    mapping(address => uint256) public balanceOf;

    event Buy(address indexed buyer, uint256 amount, uint256 cost);

    // Exponential price curve: P(x) = initialPrice * e^(growthRate * totalSupply)
    function priceToMint(uint256 _amount) public view returns (uint256) {
        return initialPrice * exp(growthRate * totalSupply);
    }

    // Simplified exponential function approximation
    function exp(uint256 x) internal pure returns (uint256) {
        return (2.71828**x);  // Simplified exponential function
    }

    // Buy tokens using ETH
    function buy() public payable {
        uint256 tokenAmount = msg.value / priceToMint(1);
        totalSupply += tokenAmount;
        reserveBalance += msg.value;
        balanceOf[msg.sender] += tokenAmount;

        emit Buy(msg.sender, tokenAmount, msg.value);
    }

    // Withdraw ETH from the reserve
    function withdraw() external {
        uint256 amount = reserveBalance;
        reserveBalance = 0;
        payable(msg.sender).transfer(amount);
    }
}
c. Logarithmic Bonding Curve

In a logarithmic bonding curve, the price increases steeply in the beginning but flattens out over time. This model is suitable when early buyers need to be rewarded, but later buyers should still have a chance to enter without extreme price increases.

